    male|a||Employment is the awkward one, because a job ties a person to an employer, so the datastore also carries units of its own for enterprises and establishments.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||Households and families are units in their own right as well — but nothing here is a municipality: KOMMNR_FORMELL is a number carried by a person, not a unit with rows of its own.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||Which is why an import line carries two things: a variable from one of these registers, and a date.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||That date has to land inside the variable's span — ask for wage income in 1985, the year our cohort was born, and the blank cells up there are your answer.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||A quick check before we open a variable entry. In the datastore, what is one row of BEFOLKNING_KJOENN?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||One person — the person is the default unit, and household or firm units have to be asked for separately.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||Three things settled before you write a line of script: which register holds the variable, which years it covers, and what one of its rows is.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Now that we have seen which registers and which years the datastore covers, let's read one catalogue card line by line — because every mistake in our gender-gap script starts with a card someone skimmed.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||Unit type: person. That one word fixes the shape of everything you build — one row per human being, not per household, not per firm.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||Data type: categorical, and the values are stored as bare codes, not as words.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||So the card has to tell you what the codes mean: one is man, two is woman.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Nothing in the data itself ever says "woman" — the meaning lives here, in the metadata, and filtering on the wrong side of that line silently hands you the other half of Norway.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||And time coverage: sex is a fixed variable, one value per person for all time, so the import takes no date at all — while an income card shows a span of years and demands one.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Now look at the name once more, because it is not only a name.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||Everything before the first underscore is the register the variable comes from: BEFOLKNING is the population register, which is event-based, so it can answer for any date you ask.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||INNTEKT comes from the tax settlement, one value per income year; NUDB is the national education database, which likewise lands once a year.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||So the prefix tells you the update rhythm before you read a single number — and all of these cards are public: you can browse the catalogue at microdata dot no long before you have an account.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||Now the card becomes script: version fifty-four of the datastore, and an empty dataset of persons.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||The card said fixed, so this import carries no date — the catalogue name on the left, your short alias, sex, on the right.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||Wage income is the other kind: its card covers a span of years, so we must name a date, the first of January twenty twenty-two.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Tabulate, and back come exactly the two codes the card promised — one and two, with no words attached, because the labels never left the metadata.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||A quick check. A card says the variable is fixed. What does your import line need?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||No date — a fixed variable has one value per person, so you import it bare; only time-varying variables take a date.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||A variable card is a contract: the unit type says what a row is, the codes say what you may filter on, and the prefix says how often the truth behind it is refreshed.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||Now that we have seen how a single variable is described in the catalogue, let''s ask the datastore for a whole cohort of people: everyone born in 1985.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||One dataset, one row per person — and two variables that need no date at all, because sex and birth month are fixed for life.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Birth month arrives as a six-digit number like 198504, so dividing by a hundred and truncating leaves the year, and then we keep only 1985.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Tabulate sex, and the cohort counts itself — though these figures come from the synthetic data the emulator runs on, never from real people.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||In the real datastore that one line picks out about fifty-one thousand people: the children born in Norway in 1985, near the bottom of the century''s fertility dip.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||The count comes out a little above the birth register, because everyone born in 1985 who immigrated later is in there too — so expect roughly fifty to sixty thousand rows.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||Here are the forty years of this cohort''s life, and the two fixed variables stretch across all of it: imported without a date, true in every year.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Municipality is not fixed — it is an annual snapshot, one value per year, carried for them from the moment they were registered.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Highest completed education, NUDB_BU, can only say something once they have finished something, so it turns informative in their late teens.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||And wage income is stamped with an income year: a summer job''s few thousand kroner first, then a working life.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||They turned eighteen in 2003 and twenty-five in 2010, and from about there all four variables carry real content at once.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||That overlap, not the register's full span, is the window our gender-gap question actually lives in.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||A quick check on the four. Which of these must be imported with a date?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Wage income is an income-year variable — without a date the datastore has no idea which year's wages you asked for.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Fifty-odd thousand people sounds like plenty of data.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Split them by sex and by five levels of education, and each group is down to about five thousand.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Add Norway's three hundred and fifty-odd municipalities, and the average cell holds fifteen people.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a|grave|That is the real constraint of register data: the datastore covers everyone, yet the moment you cross three variables the cells get thin.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||So the map is complete — registers with their years, a unit of observation, a variable entry read line by line, and now one cohort of some fifty-five thousand people with sex, municipality, education and wage waiting to be imported.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|a||Next: Building datasets I: create-dataset and the four shapes of import':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
